From 71a7eb94cb6ac613d819ce976d7a6a02480a1917 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: overdoingism <overdoingism@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 17:45:20 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] ggml-cuda: RDNA4 FA decode/verify band, fetch q8_0 K/V ahead
 into registers (bit-identical)

The band loads each K and V batch of a KV iteration synchronously: the global loads of a batch are issued only once
the previous batch has been computed and every warp has passed the barrier, then waited for at once, so every batch
exposes a full memory round trip.  A thread trace on gfx1201 (rocprofv3 --att, KV 110592) puts about 40 % of the
band's time in these load waits and 20 % at the barriers behind them.

With q8_0 K and V read in place (decode/verify, up to 8 query rows), the band now launches a kernel variant that
fetches the raw bytes of the batches (K first, then V) into registers ahead of use and only converts and stores them
to shared memory when the batch is due; when a batch is staged, the next one is fetched into the registers it freed.
Its barriers order shared memory only (__syncthreads() also waits for every outstanding global load, which would drain
the fetches at the next barrier).  The quants stay packed in 32-bit words and the conversion is pinned at the stage
point; otherwise the compiler unpacks the bytes right after the fetch.

- 64-column block (query widths 5..8, 2 chunks per thread and batch): the first two batches of an iteration are
  fetched at its start, two batches stay in flight, Q stays in registers.
- 32-column block (widths 1..4, 4 chunks per thread and batch): one batch in flight, and the stream continues across
  the end of an iteration into the next one (the caller fetches the first batch and finds the next KV block that is not
  fully masked).  Q is read from shared memory: a register-resident Q is spilled here, and its scratch reloads count
  in the same load counter as the fetched K/V, so every reload would wait for the fetch-ahead.

The variant is a kernel of its own (flash_attn_ext_f16<..., band_fetch_ahead = true>), compiled in two new translation
units (template-instances/fattn-mma-f16-band-fetch-ahead-*.cu, from generate_cu_files.py).  Every other launch,
including f16 / bf16 K/V and prefill, runs the kernel without it, and GGML_HIP_FA_BAND_FETCH_AHEAD=0 runs it for q8_0
too.  Next to each other in one translation unit the two kernels compile differently (the compiler optimizes the code
they share differently, same results); in separate units, the kernels without the variant compile to the same gfx1201
instructions as before this change (apart from source line numbers in device asserts), and the existing q8_0 loader
is unchanged.

The staged tiles hold the same values in the same slots, so the result is bit-identical: test-backend-ops
FLASH_ATTN_EXT dumps (q8_0 474/474, head 256 all K/V types 962/962, long-KV band cases at GQA 6 and 8 195/195) match
plain r92 bit for bit, with the variant and with GGML_HIP_FA_BAND_FETCH_AHEAD=0.  gfx1201, head 256, q8_0 K/V,
KV 110592 / 32768: GQA 6 widths 1..4 -13 % / -19 %, widths 5..8 -14 % / -10 %; GQA 8 widths 1..4 -13 % / -13 %,
5..8 -12 % / -9 %.
f16 / bf16 and the switch off: same as plain r92.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
